{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\": \"submission.json\", \"summary\": \"Submitted draft/submission.json (action=review) for the accepted third scenario of obligation c-at-most-one-leader-per-term, artifact a0f6ac6ed3084e05bd53e23a7051a8cf (version 1). The submission supplies the required checker_correspondence item, the applicability item and a resolution for the inherited input issue a025d4c58b334a8b8238edc1e218543c, which applies to this artifact because the earlier resolution covered the first scenario's lineage only. The correspondence item (no_issue_found, 7 cited ranges, 5 retained limitations) states that this artifact is the third scenario of the same requirement rather than a new proposition — it asks whether the overlap persists, which the earlier review left open — and reviews the fixed inputs, oracle, correlation, observation and excluded explanations together: the overlap harness plus the fabric block that keeps the two leaders apart and a window in which both nodes run with the third voter reachable; applicability requires the first node to still lead the admitted term and the second node's published term to be that same term, so a node that moved to another term is never judged; the admission event records the sitting leader's claim when the overlap was created and precedes the result in the single runner stream with matching identity. The retained observation is a bounded negative with a satisfied applicability: the first leader is still Leader in term 3 at the end of an eleven second window while the second node is a Follower in that same term and the third voter is also a follower, at exit 0. The excluded explanations are recorded with their sources — a higher term is impossible because the blocked peer cannot raise it and the reachable voter stayed in the same term, an unreachable peer is excluded because the block covers only the two leaders, and a transport failure is excluded because replication errors only back off — which leaves the leader's own lease check, since it counts a voter as contacted only within LeaderLeaseTimeout while a leader's contact refresh interval is derived from HeartbeatTimeout. The limitations state plainly that this is a negative result about persistence in one configuration, that the step-down is attributed to the lease by exclusion rather than by reading lease state (which is cleared on step-down), that a longer window or a lease covering the refresh interval is not exercised, that the third voter's reachability is inferred from the block, and that the instant violation of the same requirement remains confirmed by the earlier check. The applicability item (no_issue_found, 6 cited ranges, 3 limitations) argues why the duty covers the whole life of a term and not only the instant a leadership is established, notes that the lease is the implementation-side enforcement point that ended the overlap, and retains the transfer-exemption condition as an outstanding-repair boundary for the creation side. The resolution answers that condition for this artifact, cites both the window execution and the earlier overlap execution, declares no residual issues and keeps the repair location and the lease-coherence question open. With both blockers answered, the assessment for this artifact should recompute to a reviewed complete holding comparison, and the run would stand at six confirmed findings plus this bounded negative, with the frontier now limited to whether the overlap persists under a lease long enough to cover the contact refresh interval (raised as a separate question about lease coherence), whether production TCP scheduling reproduces the heartbeat interleaving, and the deferred surface about the commit context a follower leaves after installing a snapshot. Optional preflight with the trusted validator returned valid=true; the review introduces no new execution and no map change, and the two Chinese fields are presentation only.\"}"}}
